# Small tasks: just implement

User instruction overrides Superpowers process skills (brainstorming, writing-plans, executing-plans, design specs) for **small** work.

## Treat as small — implement immediately

- Copy, labels, RTL/CSS tweaks, one-screen layout fixes
- Clear bugfixes with an obvious expected result
- Wiring an existing pattern (toast, dialog, form field, report row)
- Rules, memory, config, one-liner env/docs notes
- A few files, no new product surface, no schema/RLS, no open product question

For these: **do not** write a spec, plan, or design doc; **do not** switch to plan mode; **do not** run brainstorming. Edit the code and verify.

## Still do the full process

- New screens, flows, or roles
- Schema / RLS / Edge Function / auth changes
- Ambiguous UX or more than one reasonable product choice
- Anything the user explicitly asks to design or spec first

If unsure whether it is small, implement the obvious slice and ask one question — do not start a design document.
